1. e4 c5 2. d4 cxd4 3. c3 dxc3 4. Nxc3 Nc6 5. Nf3 e6 6. Bc4 Bb4 {A remarkable
system. Black's main plan is to obtain good central control.} 7. O-O a6 8. Bf4
$6 {Pretty harmless. White should play De2 to avoid the Black's liberation
after Sg-e7 and d5} Nge7 9. Bd6 Bxd6 10. Qxd6 O-O {Black is a bit passive and
his position is cramped. But how shall White continue? His position would be
fine if his dark-squared bishop would have been still on the board} 11. Rfd1 ({
Maybe White should play} 11. h4 $5) 11... Ng6 12. b4 Qe7 13. b5 Qxd6 14. Rxd6
axb5 15. Bxb5 f5 $1 {Black starts counterplay. White tries to strenghten his
position, but has no real attacking targets.} 16. a4 fxe4 17. Nxe4 Nge7 18. Rc1
Nd5 19. Nc3 ({A dull retreat, very strong seemed to be} 19. Nc5) 19... Nde7 20.
Re1 Nf5 {Looks like both sides have no real ideas. Black is a pawn up but his
pieces are bad coordinated and is awkward in development special his bishop}
21. Rd2 h6 22. Ne4 d5 {Finally, but this weakens the e-pawn seriously} 23. Nc5
Nd8 24. Nd4 $2 {The critical position in the game} ({Better} 24. Ne5 $1 {
with good play for White}) 24... Nxd4 25. Rxd4 Rf6 26. h3 $6 {no idea} Nc6 27.
Rd2 Na7 28. Bd7 {makes ist quite easy for Black} Bxd7 29. Nxd7 Rf7 30. Nc5 b6
31. Nxe6 Re7 {Material equality} 32. Rde2 Nc6 33. Nf4 Rxe2 34. Rxe2 d4 35. Re6
Rc8 36. Rd6 Kf8 37. Kf1 Ke7 38. Re6+ Kd7 39. Rg6 Ne5 40. Rxg7+ Kd6 {White has
won a pawn but the black d-pawn is quite advanced and dangerous to White} 41.
Rb7 Rc6 42. Rh7 Kc5 43. Ke2 Kb4 44. Nd3+ Nxd3 45. Kxd3 Kxa4 46. Kxd4 b5 47.
Ra7+ Kb3 48. f4 Rc4+ 49. Ke5 b4 50. f5 Kc2 {Who wins the race?} 51. f6 b3 52.
